Fix pause-tube space delimiter handling (issue #41; upstream bug, unfixed in
v1.13 and on master).

In prot.c, CMD_PAUSE_TUBE was defined as "pause-tube" without a trailing
space delimiter, whereas all other commands taking arguments (e.g. CMD_PUT,
CMD_STATS_TUBE, CMD_USE, CMD_WATCH) include a trailing space in their
macro definition (e.g. "stats-tube ", "use ").

Because CMD_PAUSE_TUBE lacked the trailing space:
1. which_cmd() matched any command line starting with "pause-tube",
   such as `pause-tubedefault 5\r\n`.
2. dispatch_cmd() forwarded c->cmd + CMD_PAUSE_TUBE_LEN directly to
   read_tube_name(), which parsed the tube name starting immediately
   at offset 10 ("default 5"), without requiring or checking for a
   space delimiter.
3. read_duration() parsed the delay, and the tube was paused, replying
   PAUSED\r\n instead of rejecting the command as UNKNOWN_COMMAND\r\n.

Per the protocol specification (doc/protocol.txt), commands start with a
single-word command name, and commands and arguments are separated by a space.
Define CMD_PAUSE_TUBE as "pause-tube " so that a space delimiter is required
between the command name and the tube argument, matching the convention of
all other argument-taking commands.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@
 #define CMD_LIST_TUBES_WATCHED "list-tubes-watched"
 #define CMD_STATS_TUBE "stats-tube "
 #define CMD_QUIT "quit"
-#define CMD_PAUSE_TUBE "pause-tube"
+#define CMD_PAUSE_TUBE "pause-tube "
 
 #define CONSTSTRLEN(m) (sizeof(m) - 1)
